---
title: "TESSERA v1.1: more stable embeddings, fewer artefacts"
date: 2026-06-09
tags: [announcement, release]
author: "Anil Madhavapeddy and Frank Feng on behalf of team TESSERA"
description: "v1.1 embeddings are more temporally stable year-on-year and have far fewer artefacts in low-observation areas, and nothing in your workflow has to change."
---

We're pleased to announce the availability of TESSERA v1.1, a retrained
successor to our original v1.0 model.  If you use TESSERA embeddings for any
downstream tasks such as classification, change detection, regression, then the
new embeddings are drop-in replacements that will improve accuracy. Your code
and workflow stay exactly the same; the only step is to re-download the
embeddings for your region as v1.1 and re-run.

## What's better for your downstream tasks

- More **temporal stability** year-on-year. v1.1 128-dim embeddings are more
  consistent across years for the same location. That makes multi-year work such
  as change detection, trend analysis, or training a classifier on one year and
  applying it to another, much more reliable.
- **Fewer artefacts in sparse or imbalanced observation areas.** v1.0 could
  sometimes produce noisy tiles in places with few clear satellite observations
  due to persistent cloud, short time series, or sensor gaps. It could also show
  boundary-like artefacts where observation counts changed abruptly across
  acquisition or tiling boundaries—for example, along Sentinel-1 ASC/DESC
  coverage edges where one side of a boundary may have ~100 valid observations
  and the other ~150. v1.1 handles both sparse and highly imbalanced observation
  patterns more gracefully, so quality holds up in the places that formerly
  showed issues. Note that this only affected large-scale downstream tasks; if
  you used a small ROI that didn't straddle problematic tiles, there will be no
  change.
- **Improved coastal coverage** v1.0's land mask was too aggressive and dropped
  legitimate land pixels in coastal areas. We've listened to our mangrove-loving
  friends and extended the training and inference buffer to 20km to cover
  coastlines and remote islands.

